<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Tauromenium</h1><p class="st-meta">358 BCE – 212 BCE (approx.) · capital Tauromenium</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek city on a terrace above the Ionian Sea, founded by survivors of Naxos, famous for its theatre with a view of Etna.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a> in 403 BCE, its survivors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicels">Sicels</a> settled on Monte Tauro above the old site. In 358 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andromachus-of-tauromenium">Andromachus</a>, father of the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timaeus-of-tauromenium">Timaeus</a>, refounded the community as a Greek city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Tauromenium</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andromachus-of-tauromenium">Andromachus</a> the city welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> when he landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 344 BCE to free <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Tauromenium</a> later passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse">Agathocles</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-ii-of-syracuse">Hieron II of Syracuse</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> and enlarged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, overlooks the coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Mount Etna</a>. Inscriptions record its magistrates and public finances in detail.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a> in 212 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Tauromenium</a> came under Roman rule, enjoying the privileged status of an allied city. Octavian later made it a colony after it sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sextus-pompey">Sextus Pompeius</a>. It survives as Taormina.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tauromenium.jpg" alt="Tauromenium" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Carsten Steger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_image_of_the_coast_of_Taormina_(view_from_the_southeast).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>358 BCE – 212 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tauromenium</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Andromachus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tauromenion, Taormina (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-285&amp;focus=tauromenium" aria-label="Tauromenium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5061 817 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tauromenium lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5381 1073L5381 1074M5381 1073L5383 1099M5381 1073L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 1074h0M5383 1099h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 1073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-285&amp;focus=tauromenium">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
